<section class="reason">
  <p class="num">REASON 01</p>
  <h2>Sleeping more was never going to fix it — and being told to is the most useless advice in the category</h2>
  <p>Every man with this problem has done the experiment. Eight hours a night, consistently, for weeks. It helps the morning puffiness a bit. It does nothing about the shadow or the texture underneath, and by day ten you work out that "get more sleep" was not a diagnosis — it was a way of ending the conversation.</p>
  <p>So you accept it. And the acceptance is expensive, because the thing keeps talking on your behalf: at work, on calls, at home, in every first impression you make before you have opened your mouth.</p>
  <div class="pull">"I got eight hours. My boss asked me on a morning call if I'd been up all night."</div>
  <p>The reason sleep does not fix it is that fatigue was rarely the cause. What you are looking at is some combination of fluid, thinning skin, and lost firmness in the thinnest skin on your body — none of which sleep repairs. Cebeam works on those directly.</p>
</section>

<!-- 2 -->
<section class="reason">
  <p class="num">REASON 02</p>
  <h2>Your face is briefing the room before you say anything</h2>
  <p>At thirty-two, looking rough reads as working hard. At fifty-four it reads as slowing down, and slowing down is the specific thing that gets you quietly routed around on the projects that matter. Nobody announces it. They just stop asking.</p>
  <p>Then there is the domestic version, which is worse. You get read as annoyed, checked-out, or unapproachable when you are none of those, and you spend energy correcting an impression you never created.</p>
  <div class="pull">"I look angry even when I'm perfectly happy. My daughter asked why I was mad at her. I wasn't."</div>
  <p>The shadow under the eye is what the brain reads as fatigue and irritation — it is a hardwired signal. Reduce the shadow and you stop transmitting it. That is the whole return on this: your face stops filing reports you did not authorise.</p>
</section>

<!-- 3 -->
<section class="reason">
  <p class="num">REASON 03</p>
  <h2>Until now there was nothing between a $45 bottle and surgery</h2>
  <p>This is the real reason so many men do nothing for years. Women with this problem can at least cover it — there is an entire industry for that. For men there are two options and a canyon between them.</p>
  <p>On one side, an eye cream in a black bottle that costs <strong>$45</strong> and does approximately nothing, because a cream cannot get past the skin's barrier layer in any meaningful amount. On the other, lower eyelid surgery at <strong>$3,000–$6,000</strong>, general anaesthetic, and <strong>seven to ten days</strong> where you cannot turn up to work.</p>
  <div class="pull">"I'm not taking two weeks off and coming back with a different face. There's no version of that conversation I want to have."</div>
  <p>Cebeam is <mark>$XX</mark>, takes fifteen seconds, and costs you no days off. It is the middle option that did not exist five years ago.</p>
