<section class="reason">
  <p class="num">REASON 04</p>
  <h2>The reason creams fail is delivery, not ingredients</h2>
  <p>The outer layer of your skin exists to keep things out. It is a barrier, it is extremely good at its job, and it does not make an exception because you spent money. Most of any cream you apply never gets through it — some sits on top and ends up on the pillowcase, some evaporates, and a fraction makes it in.</p>
  <p><mark>[CITATION A — barrier function]</mark> <em>One sentence with the figure and the source named inline. Same source as Slot 1 on the advertorial.</em></p>
  <p>Under the eye it is worse, because that skin is the thinnest on the body — so eye products get deliberately formulated weak, or they would sting. A weak formula against a barrier built to block it.</p>
  <p>Cebeam's cones are made from the actives themselves, dried and compressed, and they start on the other side of that barrier. <mark>[CITATION B — microneedle vs topical delivery]</mark> <em>Comparative figure, source named inline. Same source as Slot 3. This is your strongest number — repeat it in the ads.</em></p>
</section>

<!-- 5 -->
<section class="reason">
  <p class="num">REASON 05</p>
  <h2>It is not a routine, and nobody has to know you use it</h2>
  <p>Most men will not adopt anything that requires steps, and they are right not to — a routine survives about nine days before it becomes a shelf of things you feel vaguely guilty about.</p>
  <p>This is the whole method: press one patch under each eye before bed, peel them off in the morning. Two nights a week. No cleanser step, no serum, nothing to layer, nothing to rinse.</p>
  <p>It is also invisible. There is no colour in it and nothing sits on your face during the day — it works overnight and it is gone by breakfast. Nobody at work sees a product, and there is nothing on the bathroom shelf that needs explaining.</p>
</section>

<!-- 6 -->
<section class="reason">
  <p class="num">REASON 06</p>
  <h2>If your bags are the inherited kind, this will not fix them — and we would rather say so</h2>
  <p>Read this bit properly, because it is the honest answer rather than the one that sells more units.</p>
  <p>If the fullness comes from <strong>inherited fat pads</strong> — the kind visible in photographs of you at twenty-five, the kind your father and your brother also have — no patch, cream or serum shifts those. Only surgery does. Same goes for deep hollows set by bone structure.</p>
  <p>What does respond is everything else: <strong>fluid, thinning skin, lost firmness, and the crepey texture that catches hard overhead light.</strong> That covers most men who end up on a page like this.</p>
  <p>Most people are a mix of both, which is exactly why two men the same age get very different results from the same product. If you are mostly the first kind, keep your money.</p>
</section>

